      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Mainly bacteriostatic; bind the 50S ribosomal subunit and inhibit protein synth."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "GI upset (mainly erythromycin; less w clarithromycin/azithro; food helps). QT prolongation/torsades (erythromycin most; clarithromycin/azithro less likely to cause arrhythmia). Severe, sometimes fatal hepatotoxicity (azithromycin label). Erythromycin: tinnitus/reversible hearing loss, cholestatic jaundice; ⍉in neonates (pyloric stenosis). May worsen myasthenia gravis."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Prior cholestatic jaundice/hepatic dysfun w that macrolide. Clarithromycin: w lovastatin/simvastatin/lomitapide, w colchicine in renal or hepatic impairment, w cisapride/pimozide (fatal arrhythmias). Caution w known QT prolongation or other QT drugs."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Erythromycin and clarithromycin inhibit CYP450 (azithro least): ↑ INR w warfarin, rhabdomyolysis w lovastatin/simvastatin, somnolence w midazolam/triazolam, theophylline toxicity, ↑ tacrolimus/cyclosporine/ergots."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Take w food if GI upset. Stop and seek care for signs of hepatitis."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "GAS pharyngitis w penicillin allergy:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " azithro 12 mg/kg QD (max 500 mg) for 5 days, or clarithromycin 7.5 mg/kg/dose BID (max 250 mg/dose) for 10 days. GAS resistance to macrolides = well known and var by place and time."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
